# Fibrations Difféologiques et Homotopie (1985)

**Author:** Patrick Iglesias-Zemmour
**Type:** Thèse de Doctorat d'État
**Institution:** Université de Provence, Marseille
**Date:** November 29, 1985
**Jury:** J.-M. Souriau (Advisor), D. Bennequin, J. Breuneval, J. Dixmier (President), P. Donato, J. Pradines, R. Stora.

## 📜 Archival Status
**STATUS: HISTORICAL ARCHIVE**

This directory contains the **2025 Critical Transcription** of the original 1985 thesis. It has been transcribed from the original typewritten manuscript into modern \LaTeX{} to ensure preservation.

**⚠️ WARNING: This text is a historical document, not a current reference manual.**

## ⚠️ Avertissement / Warning (2025)

The theory of Diffeology has evolved significantly since 1985. Readers and researchers must be aware that this text contains terminology and definitions that are now considered **obsolete** or **imprecise**.

### 1. Obsolete Terminology
*   **"Groupoïde Parfait" (Perfect Groupoid):** In this text, this term denotes a transitive groupoid where the characteristic map is a subduction (a diffeological fibration). It has **no relation** to the algebraic concept of a "Perfect Group."
*   **"Valuation":** Used here to denote the **Evaluation map** ($ev(f,x) = f(x)$).
*   **"Sous-groupoïde Respectueux":** Corresponds to what is now called a **Wide Subgroupoid** (a subgroupoid containing all objects of the parent).

### 2. Mathematical Imprecision
*   **Groupoid Morphisms:** The definition provided in this thesis (and propagated to the 2013 AMS book) lacks a necessary condition regarding injectivity on objects. This affects the construction of quotient groupoids.
*   **Micro-triviality:** This condition was an early attempt to define fibrations without topology. While intuitive, the modern definition of Diffeological Fibrations is more robust.

## 📚 Canonical Reference
For all rigorous mathematical work, citations, and definitions, please refer to the **Revised Edition** of the standard monograph:

> **Patrick Iglesias-Zemmour, *Diffeology*, Beijing World Publishing Corporation.**

(A digital copy of this revised edition is available in the [2013-Diffeology](../2013-Diffeology-AMS/Beijing-2022-Edition/) directory of this archive).

